D3. Every division researches its own class, learns what its ships must train and farm and how they level, and flies mostly that work; the divisions intertwine a little where it helps. Military trains weapons and combat, the others their own (2026-11-17; rules F23, F30, F32). Every member knows its class: its guidebook opens with how its ships are built and with its skills in the order they are levelled.
D4. Expansion in three directions, the systems he names being suggestions: south (the region of Biham and Felis), north (Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba: two bases), east (Diadem, Intercrus). What we may build is limited, so nothing stands close: an outpost, a hub or a base stands 3 or 4 systems beyond our safe space, never right at its border, and 3 or 4 systems from the next one of ours, so that each also serves the exploration further out (2026-11-17; rule F33). Outposts and hubs are built freely by that rule. At the edge of our own safe space the storage for the ventures beyond it is a Faction Lockbox at a station there. The first outpost: yes.
D5. The fleet we aim for: about 500 ships (Command 51, Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60). A new pilot is born on his word only.
D6. Reports inform him and ask nothing; the Admirals write as they always did; the Fleet Admiral explains to him. He speaks when he sees something wrong or a better way.
D7. The orders evolve: only his standing orders and goals are permanent. A task leaves the orders when it is done or has become a rule.
D8. Roles (2026-11-17; rule F16): Logistics hauls, crafts and is the fleet's base builder: it builds our outposts, stations and facilities, makes what they take, hauls their fuel and supplies and keeps them running. Trade trades, and works price gaps (arbitrage) later if possible. Intel scouts, explores and hunts. Military and Mining stand as they are. Passenger lines: perhaps later, when the empire stands.
D9. Pirates (2026-11-17): we do not fight them and do not test them yet; they are too strong for us now. We will meet them eventually and raid them later, when we are stronger. Until then every ship of ours avoids them.
D10. Missions (2026-11-17; rules F34, F35): every mission a board offers is flown when it pays more than it costs; the missions of our own empire are flown by every pilot to the end of each chain, for Voidborn Mastery, also when the credits only cover the fuel. A mission that asks to destroy a pirate waits for his word (D9).
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (rules F8, F23, F30).
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work, most of each division on work of its own class.
P2. An economy that earns (rules F22, F25, F31, F34). A route gets no more ships than its bid, its field or its herd carries. The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space, what we craft, the surplus sold, and every mission that pays (about 3.7 million cr of missions that pay once stand open: the tool fleet, view=capacity, ONCE).
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour; the fleet's steady income per hour above zero.
P3. Level and upgrade (rule F17): Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open the hull tiers 2 to 5; a tier-1 hull asks no Piloting (the table `hulls`: Mining found the Liminal, Logistics the Ledger).
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its class gained and its next hull step.
P4. Outposts, hubs and bases (G3, D4, rule F33). The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates what it takes; Logistics builds, supplies and keeps it; Military guards it; Intel reads it first. (a) The first outpost stands at GSC-0044 (game/map/sites.tsv, chosen 2026-11-17): 3 systems beyond our border, 3 jumps from The Experiment through Achernar and Cedarhold, 6 from home; two belts of iron and copper of 100,000 each; lanes to Acrux, Atlas, Botein, Cedarhold, HD 40307 and Intercrus, toward Diadem in the east; no pirate on our record in it or one jump away. Mining and Logistics named it; Garnet, which Intel and Military named, is 2 systems out and 2 jumps from it. Its credits are in the builder's wallet: 174,940 cr for the kit (two on offer at about 74,010 cr at Ironlight Crossroads, lhs_1140) and the fee of 100,000 cr. An outpost stands at a place without a station in lawless space; it brings faction storage and a fuel bunker, costs no rent, serves members only and has 12,000 hull and no shield (game/rules/09-factions.md; not flown yet). (b) Intel reads the three regions and the roads to them; the divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay. (c) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr; the reserve gathers toward it and is never sold (rule F24).
  Goal: the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; the routes around it; systems read in the three regions.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own. Two Faction Lockboxes stand, at Node Beta Industrial Station and at The Experiment Research Station; their rent, 138 cr a cycle or about 11,900 cr a day, comes from the treasury (44,034 cr at 09:40 UTC), which she keeps filled. The builder, a hauler with the faction role Builder (manage_facilities and manage_bases only), reached Corporation Management 3 with its first build: it may build every office up to the Recruitment Center. Members: 18 of the fleet's 246. The cap is not known: `members_limit` in the faction's info is the page size of its member list (the game's help), so the 50 read before was no cap; a join refused as full names the cap. The haulers join first, until one is refused. Then the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk together at Node Beta: 375,000 cr, 275 steel plate, 125 circuit boards; the last boards by the gold way (requests ENT-211). The whole ladder: governance/empire.md.
  Goal: members up to the cap, and the cap read; the treasury's days of rent; circuit boards held against 125.
TURN (the tasks of now; each leaves the orders when it is done)
Task 1. Mining and Intel: your guidebook opens with the Class Card (rule F14, D3), short and lean, before the Standing Plan: `### Ships`, how your class builds its ships (hull by tier, fit, style, in a few lines), and `### Skills`, one table of the skills of your class in the order they are levelled, the most important first (skill, why it matters, what trains it and by which of your routes, your division's level now, the next goal). Your levelling guides already hold the research; the card is its summary. Trade, Logistics and Military have theirs (read by the Fleet Admiral at 10:00 UTC).
  Done when: the cards stand at the top of mining.md and intel.md.
Task 2. Missions (D10, rules F34 and F35). Each Admiral reads the mission candidates of its class (the tool candidates with kind=mission; rule F34 says which class owns which kind) and tests each new one with one ship (rule F12), writing its route when it pays; Trade keeps the missions that pay once. The chains of our empire's missions stand in the list First of every plan, one route per link with `requires.booked` its link before; a link that stands still for the fleet is named with what opens it (The Collective Provides waits on nickel ore: Mining reads where it regrows, the route is Trade's; queue.log ENT-158).
  Done when: your report names each mission of your class you tested, what it paid or what it lacks, and the routes you wrote.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by. You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play). Plan your division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division"; sell what your ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes; build what you lack (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); answer the requests addressed to you; claim what you fill (admirals.log, rule F25). Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, judge your own routes, levels and results before you report them, and name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better and one you do not master yet.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F35); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13), the reserve (F24) and where we build (F33). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the empire with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through. Every turn: read the Admirals' reports and Feedback and fix what they name or open the door; answer every request before their next turn; keep the database, the catalog, the rules digest, the money figures, the tools and the station right; write the fleet report and lean orders (D7). Pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5) and keep the treasury filled; choose where each outpost and base stands and allocate what it takes, which Logistics then builds (P4, rules F31 and F33); check every guidebook's Class Card; read how many ships hold each level of Voidborn Mastery (rule F35); decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date. Bring to him only what is his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. The command group levels, upgrades and earns like every division (rule F30).
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; faction members against the cap, and the cap read; the treasury's days of rent; the first outpost founded; Class Cards in five guidebooks; the orders no longer than the entry before, or the reason in its `Changed:` line.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then iron. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen through `lift_to`. (1) Give 90 gold ore of Mining's stores at The Experiment Research Station into the storage of Logistics' builder there (requests ENT-211: one miner holds 100 there), at the end of a run, as Logistics' staging gives goods; say in queue.log when it landed. (2) The first outpost stands at GSC-0044 (P4 a): when it is founded, its storage takes ore without the trip home; plan the gather fields one jump around it (Botein, Acrux, Atlas, Intercrus and its own two belts). (3) The Liminal, the tier-1 miner you found: price it and test one hull inside rule F12. (4) Nickel ore for The Collective Provides (rule F35): read where it regrows and how many pilots a field carries.
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes; the gold given.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, keep the standing sell order you test with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Price gaps between two stations are yours to work as the books allow (D8): read both ends within the hour, one ship and one unit first. Missions (D10, rule F34): you keep the missions that pay once for the fleet; about 3.7 million cr of them stand open (the tool fleet, view=capacity, ONCE): for each, its route or what it lacks; the route of The Collective Provides is yours (queue.log ENT-158). Answer requests ENT-208 (Intel: voidborn-selling buys a good that has no bid). The hauling of fuel and supplies for our bases is Logistics'; what a base must buy, you buy.
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; once-missions flown and credits collected; traders at work, 40 of 40; crafting XP of the traders.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: you are the fleet's quartermaster and its base builder (D8, rule F16): you build our outposts, stations and facilities, make what they take, haul their fuel and supplies and keep them running. The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates the credits and materials (rule F33); inside that the build is yours, by routes and scripts. (1) The builder is yours again since 09:58 UTC: a member of the faction with the role Builder (manage_facilities, manage_bases), Corporation Management 3, 187,193 cr in its wallet for the outpost. The Lockbox at The Experiment stands. (2) Found the first outpost at GSC-0044 (P4 a) by your routes: the kit from Ironlight Crossroads (switch your kit route on; ask Military for a guard on its loaded jumps), then to GSC-0044; read `base_cost` at the place before the fee; `deploy_outpost` by the builder; then test with one hauler and one hold how fuel goes into its bunker. Your report names its place, its base id and what it cost. (3) The offices: the last boards by the gold way (requests ENT-211: Mining gives 90 gold ore into the builder's storage at The Experiment; the energy crystal from your own haulers' 449 at Node Beta), beside the 65 from silicon; the builder builds the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk at Node Beta when 125 boards and 275 plate stand there. (4) Keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral (the tool stock with view=reserve). A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Your haulers join the faction first (P5).
Measure: the first outpost founded, with its place and cost; circuit boards held against 125; inputs of the reserve with a known source; haulers at work, 40 of 40; later: bases built, and bases supplied without a gap.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (rule F23). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16): up to 45 ships of all divisions gather at Schedar Belt and up to 45 at Ironhollow's two belts. What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Plan the guard of the first outpost at GSC-0044 and of its kit's road from Ironlight Crossroads (P4 a), with Logistics. Pirates: no test, no group and no fight for now (D9): the pirate group your report of 08:35 UTC planned stays withdrawn (ENT-034); no drone joins a game fleet for it, a hunt leaves a place where a pirate stands, a guard leaves when one attacks, and no fit is bought for a pirate group. What the census and the public record show of them stays on file (observe.py), so that our ships avoid them and we know them when we are stronger. Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations. Read the first runs of your hunts under `alone` "pirates": a stranger who fires sends that place back to the strict rule.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour (surplus.py reserve names them); drones at work, 50 of 50, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand, from the readings alone.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time, on templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to every hour, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. For your turns of 11:15 and 15:15 UTC (Suggi, 2026-11-17): ten scouts explore as far out from our safe space as they can reach, in every direction, and read every system on the way and at the far end, each read filed the same turn; the other twenty do the normal work. After those two turns about ten explore again, the three regions of D4 and the roads to them first (rule F26). Your scouts hunt as well (D8): where a herd lies on a scout's way it hunts, inside rule F9. Read GSC-0044 and its six neighbours again before the outpost is founded (P4 a): who is there, where pirates stand, the road from The Experiment. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: a field of uranium ore first (every station bids 600 cr for it; requests ENT-194), then caustic sac and silicon ore. Write down where pirates stand, for Military (observe.py add pirate ...): your scouts look and leave (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn; the stance of rule F2 holds for them. The survey test with one scanner (your plan) decides whether a survey shows what get_poi does not.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, 10 of 30, and for the two turns the farthest system read in each direction and the systems read for the first time; GSC-0044 and its neighbours read before the founding.

## [ENT-036] | 2026-11-17 10:22 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/empire.md | see: ENT-035
Changed: the same turn of 09:30 UTC, one finding later: the faction's cap is 20 members, read from the game's refusal of a join at 10:20 UTC ("faction_full: This faction has reached its maximum of 20 members"); 20 stand now. The next step of the ladder is therefore the Hiring Board alone (cap 50), which takes only 75,000 cr, 75 steel plate and 25 circuit boards: P5, Apollo's point (3) and my Measure say so. Everything else stands as in ENT-035. The same length as ENT-035.
