Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; the Hiring Board built and faction members against its cap of 50; the treasury's days of rent; the first outpost founded; Class Cards in five guidebooks; the orders no longer than the entry before, or the reason in its `Changed:` line.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then iron. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen through `lift_to`. (1) Give 90 gold ore of Mining's stores at The Experiment Research Station into the storage of Logistics' builder there (requests ENT-211: one miner holds 100 there), at the end of a run, as Logistics' staging gives goods; say in queue.log when it landed. (2) The first outpost stands at GSC-0044 (P4 a): when it is founded, its storage takes ore without the trip home; plan the gather fields one jump around it (Botein, Acrux, Atlas, Intercrus and its own two belts). (3) The Liminal, the tier-1 miner you found: price it and test one hull inside rule F12. (4) Nickel ore for The Collective Provides (rule F35): read where it regrows and how many pilots a field carries.
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes; the gold given.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, keep the standing sell order you test with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Price gaps between two stations are yours to work as the books allow (D8): read both ends within the hour, one ship and one unit first. Missions (D10, rule F34): you keep the missions that pay once for the fleet; about 3.7 million cr of them stand open (the tool fleet, view=capacity, ONCE): for each, its route or what it lacks; the route of The Collective Provides is yours (queue.log ENT-158). Answer requests ENT-208 (Intel: voidborn-selling buys a good that has no bid). The hauling of fuel and supplies for our bases is Logistics'; what a base must buy, you buy.
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; once-missions flown and credits collected; traders at work, 40 of 40; crafting XP of the traders.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: you are the fleet's quartermaster and its base builder (D8, rule F16): you build our outposts, stations and facilities, make what they take, haul their fuel and supplies and keep them running. The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates the credits and materials (rule F33); inside that the build is yours, by routes and scripts. (1) The builder is yours again since 09:58 UTC: a member of the faction with the role Builder (manage_facilities, manage_bases), Corporation Management 3, 187,193 cr in its wallet for the outpost. The Lockbox at The Experiment stands. (2) Found the first outpost at GSC-0044 (P4 a) by your routes: the kit from Ironlight Crossroads (switch your kit route on; ask Military for a guard on its loaded jumps), then to GSC-0044; read `base_cost` at the place before the fee; `deploy_outpost` by the builder; then test with one hauler and one hold how fuel goes into its bunker. Your report names its place, its base id and what it cost. (3) The offices: the faction's cap is 20 and stands full. The Hiring Board comes first: the builder builds it at Node Beta as soon as 25 circuit boards and 75 steel plate stand there (the 65 boards from silicon cover it; the fee of 75,000 cr comes from the builder's wallet, and I top it up when the outpost has taken its share). Then the Recruitment Desk (200 plate, 100 boards): the last boards by the gold way (requests ENT-211: Mining gives 90 gold ore into the builder's storage at The Experiment; the energy crystal from your own haulers' 449 at Node Beta). (4) Keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral (the tool stock with view=reserve). A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Your haulers join the faction first (P5).
Measure: the first outpost founded, with its place and cost; the Hiring Board built; circuit boards held against 25, then 100; inputs of the reserve with a known source; haulers at work, 40 of 40; later: bases built, and bases supplied without a gap.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (rule F23). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16): up to 45 ships of all divisions gather at Schedar Belt and up to 45 at Ironhollow's two belts. What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Plan the guard of the first outpost at GSC-0044 and of its kit's road from Ironlight Crossroads (P4 a), with Logistics. Pirates: no test, no group and no fight for now (D9): the pirate group your report of 08:35 UTC planned stays withdrawn (ENT-034); no drone joins a game fleet for it, a hunt leaves a place where a pirate stands, a guard leaves when one attacks, and no fit is bought for a pirate group. What the census and the public record show of them stays on file (observe.py), so that our ships avoid them and we know them when we are stronger. Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations. Read the first runs of your hunts under `alone` "pirates": a stranger who fires sends that place back to the strict rule.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour (surplus.py reserve names them); drones at work, 50 of 50, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand, from the readings alone.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time, on templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to every hour, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. For your turns of 11:15 and 15:15 UTC (Suggi, 2026-11-17): ten scouts explore as far out from our safe space as they can reach, in every direction, and read every system on the way and at the far end, each read filed the same turn; the other twenty do the normal work. After those two turns about ten explore again, the three regions of D4 and the roads to them first (rule F26). Your scouts hunt as well (D8): where a herd lies on a scout's way it hunts, inside rule F9. Read GSC-0044 and its six neighbours again before the outpost is founded (P4 a): who is there, where pirates stand, the road from The Experiment. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: a field of uranium ore first (every station bids 600 cr for it; requests ENT-194), then caustic sac and silicon ore. Write down where pirates stand, for Military (observe.py add pirate ...): your scouts look and leave (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn; the stance of rule F2 holds for them. The survey test with one scanner (your plan) decides whether a survey shows what get_poi does not.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, 10 of 30, and for the two turns the farthest system read in each direction and the systems read for the first time; GSC-0044 and its neighbours read before the founding.
